#
# The legal review is a document about a *person*, not about the protocol. It
# names the founder and the jurisdiction whose law applies to him, because that
# is what makes a lawyer's opinion mean anything -- and publishing it would
# waive privilege, invite third-party reliance, and tie a private individual to
# the project permanently and irreversibly. It stays with him.
docs/LEGAL_REVIEW_BRIEF_AR.md
docs/LEGAL_REVIEW_BRIEF_BILINGUAL.md
out/

# The key ceremony runbook describes how the founder generates and stores a key
# on an air-gapped machine. It contains no key, and publishing it would show
# real rigour -- but it is operational detail about one person's security, and
# it is far easier to publish later than to unpublish.
docs/FOUNDER_KEY_RUNBOOK.md

# The working log. Honest to a fault: every bug, every wrong turn, machine
# paths and local addresses. Valuable internally, not a deliverable, and a
# ready-made list of where to look for the next weakness.
PROGRESS.md

# Correspondence with a named reviewer. His to publish, not ours.
review/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Anchored to the repository root, and the leading slash is the whole point.
# Unanchored, `build/` matches a directory of that name at ANY depth -- which
# silently swallowed site/build/, the page generated from docs/BUILD.md. The
# file existed on disk, the generator reported success, /start/ linked to it
# and the sitemap advertised it, and it would have gone live as a 404 because
# git never tracked it. Found by checking `git ls-files` against what was on
# disk rather than trusting that a written file is a published file.
#
# Anchoring loses nothing: /build/ still hides the 5,050-file tree at the root
# and build/randomx/build inside it, and the two other build directories have
# their own patterns below.
/build/
depends/
*.o
*.a
*.so
*.dylib
*.dll
*.node
node_modules/
pool/native/build/
pool/logs/

